# Your personalized GenAI roadmap

Target: AI/ML Engineer with GenAI specialization

My recommendation is to build on your existing ML and software engineering experience and progressively add deep learning, LLMs, and generative modeling.

I would design your learning path around one objective:

Become an AI/ML Engineer who can develop, understand, evaluate, and deploy both traditional ML models and GenAI systems.

Not just someone who knows how to call an LLM API.

## 1. Your starting point

Let's distinguish what you already know from what you need to strengthen.

| Area                 | Your current foundation      | Recommendation                         |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Python               | Good practical experience    | Continue using it                      |
| Mathematics          | Applied ML knowledge         | Strengthen linear algebra and calculus |
| Classical ML         | XGBoost, MLP, forecasting    | Maintain and deepen                    |
| Data processing      | Pandas, NumPy, EDA           | Already sufficient to start            |
| Software engineering | APIs, Docker, Redis          | Strong advantage                       |
| MLOps                | Training/inference pipelines | Extend toward LLMOps                   |
| Deep learning        | Some MLP experience          | Strengthen significantly               |
| PyTorch              | Needs focused development    | Learn properly                         |
| NLP                  | Limited foundation           | Start from fundamentals                |
| Transformers         | New learning area            | Study in depth                         |

You don't need to repeat introductory Python, basic statistics, or classical ML courses.

# 2. The roadmap: 16 weeks

I would allocate approximately 10–15 hours per week, alongside your current job.

