## Stage 1 — Deep Learning and PyTorch

Weeks 1–4

Foundation

Objective: Understand how modern neural networks learn and become comfortable with PyTorch.

### Week 1: Neural network fundamentals

Learn:

- Perceptrons and multilayer neural networks.
- Forward propagation.
- Backpropagation.
- Computational graphs.
- Gradient descent.
- Loss functions.
- Activation functions.

Mathematics to study alongside:

- Partial derivatives.
- Chain rule.
- Matrix multiplication.
- Gradients.

Practical exercise: implement a small neural network using NumPy, including forward and backward propagation.

### Week 2: PyTorch fundamentals

Learn:

- Tensors and tensor operations.
- Autograd.
- `torch.nn`.
- `nn.Module`.
- Optimizers.
- Loss functions.
- GPU acceleration.

Practical exercise: implement a neural network using PyTorch to classify a dataset.

### Week 3: Training deep learning models

Learn:

- Dataset and DataLoader.
- Training and validation loops.
- Batch sizes.
- Learning rates.
- Adam and AdamW.
- Weight decay.
- Dropout.
- Batch normalization versus layer normalization.

Practical exercise: train and evaluate an MLP, experiment with hyperparameters, and compare results.

### Week 4: Deep learning architecture concepts

Learn:

- CNNs and their purpose.
- RNNs and LSTMs.
- Sequence modeling.
- Word embeddings.
- Why recurrent architectures have limitations.

You don't need to master CNNs or LSTMs. Understand their design and purpose so you can appreciate why Transformers became important.

Stage 1 deliverable: A clean PyTorch project with a training pipeline, validation, metrics, checkpoints, and reproducible experiments.

Resources:

- [Official PyTorch tutorials](https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [Dive into Deep Learning](https://d2l.ai/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — particularly useful for mathematical explanations and implementations.

## Stage 2 — NLP and Transformers

Weeks 5–8

Core architecture

Objective: Understand the architecture behind modern LLMs.

### Week 5: NLP fundamentals

Learn:

- Text preprocessing.
- Tokenization.
- Vocabulary.
- One-hot encoding.
- Word2Vec.
- Word embeddings.
- Semantic similarity.
- Positional information.

Practical exercise: build a simple text classification model using PyTorch.

### Week 6: Attention mechanisms

This is an extremely important week.

Learn:

- Sequence-to-sequence models.
- Encoder-decoder architecture.
- Attention mechanisms.
- Self-attention.
- Query, Key, Value.
- Scaled dot-product attention.

Understand this equation:

\\[ Attention(Q,K,V)= softmax\left(\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)V \\]

Don't just memorize it. Understand:

- What Q, K, and V represent.
- Why we calculate their dot product.
- Why we divide by the square root of the key dimension.
- Why Softmax is used.
- How the output is constructed.

### Week 7: Transformer architecture

Learn:

- Multi-head attention.
- Positional encodings and embeddings.
- Feed-forward networks.
- Residual connections.
- Layer normalization.
- Encoder versus decoder.
- Causal masking.

[Transformers | João A. Leite](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/tCqeMkgoU_ntTKqvErydt7lGToYyozhKo2L54J2_q2jNQlB2T9do3cSjtl0zvM3RZ2Eas5BlP1oO-9AJgKqf7vC7At1xhqENtu80rvsaGz5DYQ-RHKIcbjV7fJi816FB6xQ5bXJ7sdtwOos8WefU8ONi6IuFgXi_IuE6Lqlddpg?purpose=inline)

[joaoaleite.github.io](https://joaoaleite.github.io/blog/2024/transformers/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

### Week 8: GPT and language model training

Learn:

- Autoregressive language modeling.
- Next-token prediction.
- Pretraining.
- Training objectives.
- Context windows.
- Temperature.
- Top-k and top-p sampling.
- Inference versus training.

Stage 2 deliverable: Implement a miniature GPT-style decoder-only Transformer using PyTorch.

You don't need billions of parameters. A small character-level language model is enough to understand the complete training and generation process.

Resources:

- [Hugging Face LLM Course](https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [Andrej Karpathy — Let's build GPT from scratch](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [Attention Is All You Need](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — original Transformer paper.

## Stage 3 — LLM Engineering and Generative AI

Weeks 9–12

Practical GenAI

Objective: Learn how to work with pretrained models and build useful GenAI applications.

At this point, you should have a reasonable understanding of how LLMs work internally.

### Week 9: Hugging Face ecosystem

Learn the core Hugging Face libraries:

- `transformers`
- `tokenizers`
- `datasets`
- `evaluate`
- `accelerate`

Understand:

- Loading pretrained models.
- Loading tokenizers.
- Text generation.
- Model inference.
- Pipeline API versus direct model usage.
- Model checkpoints.
- Model configuration.

Practical exercise: load an open-source model, generate text, experiment with decoding strategies, and compare outputs.

### Week 10: LLM adaptation and fine-tuning

Learn:

- Pretraining versus fine-tuning.
- Instruction tuning.
- Supervised fine-tuning (SFT).
- LoRA.
- QLoRA.
- Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT).
- Training datasets and evaluation.

You should understand the difference between:

| Approach           | Purpose                                             |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Prompt engineering | Guide model behavior through instructions           |
| RAG                | Give the model relevant external information        |
| Fine-tuning        | Adapt model behavior through training               |
| Pretraining        | Learn general language representations and patterns |

Practical exercise: fine-tune a small open-source language model using LoRA on a carefully prepared dataset.

### Week 11: Embeddings and semantic search

This is where your existing ML knowledge becomes particularly useful.

Learn:

- Embedding models.
- Dense vector representations.
- Cosine similarity.
- Semantic search.
- Vector indexing.
- Approximate nearest neighbors.
- Chunking strategies.
- Reranking.

For example, consider these sentences:

```
Sentence A: How can I train a machine learning model?

Sentence B: What are the steps to build an ML model?
```

Their words are different, but their meanings are similar.

An embedding model maps them into numerical vectors that can be compared using similarity measures.

Practical exercise:

Build a semantic search engine using:

- Sentence Transformers.
- Qdrant or PostgreSQL with pgvector.
- FastAPI.

### Week 12: RAG architecture

Learn:

- Document ingestion.
- PDF extraction.
- Text splitting.
- Embedding generation.
- Vector storage.
- Similarity search.
- Context retrieval.
- Prompt construction.
- Answer generation.
- Retrieval evaluation.

