## Stage 4 — Advanced GenAI, LLMOps and Production

Weeks 13–16

Engineering specialization

Objective: Move beyond building prototypes and learn to engineer reliable GenAI systems.

### Week 13: AI agents and tool calling

Learn:

- Function calling.
- Tool execution.
- Agent workflows.
- Planning and execution.
- Short-term and long-term memory.
- Multi-step reasoning workflows.
- MCP fundamentals.

Understand the difference between a normal LLM application and an agent.

For example:

A normal LLM application answers a question.

An agent can receive a request, decide which tool to use, call an API, inspect the result, and continue working toward the requested outcome.

Practical exercise: build an agent that can query a database, call an external API, and return a structured result.

### Week 14: LLM evaluation

This is one of the most important areas that distinguishes an experienced AI engineer from someone who simply builds demos.

Learn:

- Offline and online evaluation.
- Evaluation datasets.
- Groundedness.
- Faithfulness.
- Answer relevance.
- Retrieval precision and recall.
- Hallucination evaluation.
- LLM-as-a-judge and its limitations.
- Regression testing.

For RAG applications, evaluate retrieval separately from answer generation.

A model can generate a fluent answer while retrieving completely irrelevant documents.

### Week 15: LLMOps and optimization

This is where your existing MLOps experience becomes a significant advantage.

Learn:

- Model serving.
- Quantization.
- GPU memory management.
- Batching.
- Streaming inference.
- Caching.
- Latency optimization.
- Token-cost optimization.
- Observability.
- Prompt and model versioning.
- Guardrails and security.

Explore:

- vLLM.
- MLflow.
- Hugging Face TGI concepts.
- OpenTelemetry.
- RAG evaluation tools.

You already know Docker, FastAPI, Redis, and model deployment. You should focus on what changes when the model is an LLM rather than starting deployment from scratch.

### Week 16: Production project

Build an end-to-end GenAI system.

My recommendation is to combine your traditional ML and GenAI knowledge:

Intelligent ML Forecasting Assistant

Imagine extending your existing PortSense forecasting system with a natural-language interface.

[Dashboard agents in Looker | Google Cloud Blog](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/ZYhv8blGKF7GpwGljC8GzTJ6GV-0Mc72EDS4QYXDbR5C49WQLbD3TaG5a35ib11_-CUkTmGjMke5Pm1yWqXX5M_7A-9BWYvzkZKEtHUkxq_mjiAcTl2-UFoMSXbNAwmHRo1xgAQRvXtKR94aHTG6wqHRrPxoAz0PcdmNGS2FX7Y?purpose=inline)

[cloud.google.com](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/business-intelligence/dashboard-agents-in-looker/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

A user could ask:

- What is the expected import traffic tomorrow?
- Which domains experienced the highest increase this month?
- Explain the forecast for a particular domain.
- Compare actual traffic with predicted traffic.
- What historical trends might explain this result?

The architecture could look like this:

```
             User
              |
         FastAPI API
              |
         LLM Interface
              |
       Tool Orchestration
          /         \
         /           \
   ML Forecast     SQL Queries
      Service       Historical Data
         \           /
          \         /
         Result Processing
                |
        LLM Response
                |
             User
```

The important engineering principle: the LLM should not replace your forecasting model.

Your XGBoost/MLP forecasting system remains responsible for numerical predictions. The LLM acts as an interface for interpreting results, retrieving information, and orchestrating tools.

This is a good example of combining classical ML and GenAI rather than replacing one with the other.

