# 6. How to measure your progress

Don't measure progress by how many courses you've completed.

Use these milestones instead:

| Milestone     | You should be able to...                |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Deep learning | Explain and train neural networks       |
| PyTorch       | Implement a complete training loop      |
| Transformers  | Explain and implement self-attention    |
| LLMs          | Load and run pretrained models          |
| Fine-tuning   | Fine-tune a small model with LoRA       |
| RAG           | Build and evaluate a retrieval pipeline |
| Agents        | Implement tool calling and workflows    |
| LLMOps        | Deploy and monitor an LLM application   |

If you can do these things independently, you're building genuine engineering skills.

# 7. My advice about your career direction

I want to emphasize something important.

Don't position yourself as someone who has abandoned ML and started learning GenAI.

Position yourself as an AI/ML Engineer who understands:

- Classical machine learning.
- Deep learning.
- Generative modeling.
- LLM architectures.
- Retrieval systems.
- Fine-tuning.
- AI infrastructure and production deployment.

This is a strong technical combination.

Also, don't neglect your mathematical foundations. Your balanced theoretical approach is a good choice: understand the equations and architecture, implement the important components, and then use production frameworks.

# 8. Let's turn this into an actionable learning plan

I suggest we treat this as an ongoing learning project, with one stage at a time.

## Your first milestone

Start here — Week 1

Deep Learning fundamentals

Your first objectives:

- Understand perceptrons and multilayer neural networks.
- Understand forward propagation.
- Understand backpropagation.
- Understand gradient descent.
- Understand loss functions and activation functions.
- Implement a small neural network using NumPy.

Your first practical project

Implement a simple neural network from scratch using NumPy, without PyTorch or TensorFlow.

The objective is to understand how the network learns, not to achieve state-of-the-art accuracy.

